# Test: an LDP session that reached operational and then ended leaves no setup
# backoff, so Ze dials its neighbor again on the neighbor's next Hello, and
# publishes exactly one session-down for that session.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-single-owner-lifecycle-5-session-end-locals AC-1 and AC-2 --
#            startSessionForAdj's session-end callback deletes the backoff of
#            a session that reached OPERATIONAL, and publishes one SessionDown
#            for it, through the daemon on a real link.
# PREVENTS:  a session that ran for days being redialled only after 15 seconds,
#            then 30, up to 2 minutes, because its end was recorded as a failed
#            setup attempt (plan/journal/state-read-after-teardown-reset-it.md,
#            2026-10-02).
#
# WHY A .ci. internal/plugins/ldp/session_end_test.go drives the same callback
# over net.Pipe with a test dialer. This test answers whether the daemon does it
# end to end: a multicast Link Hello on a veth link, discovery, Ze's dial to TCP
# 646, the Initialization exchange, the session end, and the redial a real
# neighbor sees.
#
# HOW. `ldp/neighbor-link` runs the daemon in its own network namespace, joined
# by a veth pair to a second one that holds the neighbor's link
# (internal/test/fixture/ldp_neighbor_linux.go says why both are needed). The
# observer plugin `ldp/session-end-clears-backoff` is the neighbor: it sends
# Hellos, accepts Ze's session and brings it to operational, then closes it
# with its Hellos paused. Once `show ldp neighbor` reads `discovered` and the
# observer holds the session-down, it sends ONE Hello and requires Ze's next
# session to open within 10 seconds, below the 15-second floor a failed setup
# waits. The decision log is the second half: a session end recorded as a
# failed setup logs `session setup failed, next attempt delayed` for the
# neighbor, rejected below.
#
# The observer requires the neighbor's session events to be exactly
# session-up, session-down, session-up, each naming peer 10.99.0.1 and
# interface ldpz0. The second session is left operational and ends with the
# daemon, after the Hellos stop, so nothing dials after it.
#
# STATUS: a Linux/QEMU test. The fixture creates network namespaces and a veth
# pair, which needs root (`./le test qemu ci test test/ldp/ldp-session-end-clears-backoff.ci`).
option=needs-linux:caps=net-admin
option=env:var=ze.log.ldp:value=info
option=timeout:value=90s

tmpfs=ldp.conf:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	external ldp-neighbor {
		run "le test fixture ldp/session-end-clears-backoff netns zls$PORT-n"
		encoder json
	}
}

ldp {
	lsr-id 10.99.0.2
	transport-address 10.99.0.2
	hello-interval 5
	hello-hold-time 15
	keepalive-time 60
	interfaces ldpz0
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=le test fixture ldp/neighbor-link netns zls$PORT run ze start ldp.conf:timeout=85s
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stderr:contains=ldp: discovery started on interface
expect=stderr:contains=OK: Ze dialed 10.99.0.1:0
expect=stderr:pattern=OK: .*events \[session-up session-down session-up\]
reject=stderr:pattern=session setup failed, next attempt delayed.*peer=10\.99\.0\.1:0
reject=stderr:pattern=ZE-OBSERVER-FAIL
reject=stderr:pattern=panic
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
